@startuml 04-sequence-nightly
' =====================================================================
' NIGHTLY targets: graded on a per-submission triggered rerun.
' Traces to: FR-29..FR-36, FR-41..FR-47, CI-3, CI-4
'
' Phase 1 is the same submission run as 03. The only difference is what
' the AI phase does on a NIGHTLY assignment's submission run: it
' leaves it alone. A submission with both kinds of target goes through
' both diagrams.
' =====================================================================

title NIGHTLY targets -- graded on a per-submission rerun

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign left
skinparam maxMessageSize 220

actor Student as stud
participant "Scheduled\nWorkflow (cron)" as cron #EDE0F0
participant "Nightly Trigger\n(Playwright)" as trig #FBE6D4
participant "Gradescope" as gs #E8E8E8
box "Autograder Container" #F7F7F7
  participant "Autograder Script\n(run_autograder)" as script #FFF2CC
  participant "Instructor\nTest Suite" as ti #FFF2CC
  participant "Reporting\nLibrary" as wrap #FBE6D4
  participant "AI Grading\nPhase" as aip #D6E4F0
end box
participant "Web API" as api #DCEFD9
database "Grading DB" as db #DCEFD9
participant "LLM\nProviders" as llm #E8E8E8

== Phase 1: functional testing, no grading ==

stud -> gs : submit code
gs -> script : run_autograder
script -> ti : run the test suite
ti -> wrap : first call initialises the run
wrap -> api : POST /submissions
api -> db : upsert submission,\ninsert submission_target per registered target\nwith ai_needed = TRUE
api --> wrap : 200 {submission_id}

loop for each test
  opt this target needs no AI commentary
    ti -> wrap : ai_not_needed(target)
    wrap -> api : POST .../ai-not-needed
    api -> db : UPDATE submission_target\nSET ai_needed = FALSE
  end
  opt report the outcome, and anything worth telling the model
    ti -> wrap : add_test_result(target, status,\nfunctional_test_feedback)
    wrap -> api : POST .../test-result
    api -> db : INSERT test_result
  end
end

ti --> script : suite exits
script -> gs : write results.json\n(functional test results)

script -> aip : invoke AI phase

note over script #FFF8DC
  **The same script runs in both phases**, so it
  invokes the AI phase here too. The script has
  no idea which phase it is in, and does not need
  to.
end note

aip -> api : GET /submissions/{id}/action
api -> db : read grading_mode,\nai_authorized_at,\nai_graded_submitted_at
api --> aip : 200 {action: DEFER}

note over api #FFF8DC
  NIGHTLY, and ai_authorized_at is NULL, so the
  answer is DEFER. No LLM call and no tokens.

  completed_at stays NULL on every ai_needed row,
  which is exactly what tonight's trigger
  queries.
end note

aip -> aip : parse results.json and splice\na "feedback deferred" section
aip -> gs : write the new results.json
gs --> stud : test results now, and a note\nthat feedback arrives by\ntomorrow morning

== Phase 2: nightly -- trigger a rerun per submission ==

cron -> trig : invoke
trig -> api : POST /trigger-runs (start)
trig -> api : GET /submissions/pending-ai?mode=NIGHTLY
api -> db : SELECT submission JOIN submission_target\nJOIN assignment\nWHERE assignment.grading_mode = 'NIGHTLY'\nAND ai_needed AND completed_at IS NULL\nAND ai_graded_submitted_at IS NULL
db --> api : submission list
api --> trig : 200 {submissions[] with platform ids}

note right of api #FFF8DC
  **Restricted to NIGHTLY assignments**, and to
  rows with completed_at NULL, which means never
  attempted.

  A row the AI phase already attempted has
  completed_at set whether it succeeded or
  failed, so it will not come back tomorrow.
  Failure is terminal and reported in
  results.json, exactly as in IMMEDIATE (AD-8).

  What this query DOES pick up again is work
  that was never attempted, because the trigger
  job itself failed, or our service was down, or
  Gradescope was. That is the right line: "the
  job did not run" self-heals, "grading ran and
  failed" does not.
end note

trig -> gs : log in

loop for each submission needing AI grading
  trig -> api : POST /submissions/{id}/authorize
  api -> db : SET ai_authorized_at = now
  api --> trig : 200

  note right of trig #FFF8DC
    **Authorise BEFORE clicking.** The container
    can start and ask for its action within
    seconds, so a flag set afterwards would race
    and the rerun would DEFER again.
  end note

  trig -> gs : open the submission,\nclick "Rerun Autograder"
  gs --> trig : accepted
  trig -> api : POST /trigger-runs/{id}/record\n{platform_submission_id, ok}
end

note right of trig #FFF8DC
  Rerun is per submission, so the trigger only
  touches submissions that need work.

  Still the one fragile step: Gradescope has no
  public API, so locating a submission and
  clicking its rerun button is UI automation and
  will break when they reskin. Keep every
  selector in one file.

  Consider throttling the loop. A term's worth
  of submissions clicked as fast as Playwright
  manages looks like abuse from the outside.
end note

trig -> api : POST /trigger-runs/{id}/finish
api -> db : update trigger_run

== Phase 3: the rerun -- generate and splice the feedback ==

gs -> script : run_autograder again
script -> ti : the test suite runs again
ti --> script : suite exits
script -> gs : write results.json\n(functional test results)

note over script #FFF8DC
  The container was destroyed after Phase 1, so
  the tests must run again to produce
  results.json. This is also the defensive write
  for this run.
end note

script -> aip : invoke AI phase
aip -> api : GET /submissions/{id}/action
api -> db : read grading_mode,\nai_authorized_at,\nai_graded_submitted_at
api --> aip : 200 {action, targets[], rubric,\nfeedback_guidance, test_result[],\nproviders[]}

alt action = REEMIT (already graded)
  note over aip #FFF8DC
    **The token guard.** Reruns happen for
    reasons other than our trigger: an instructor
    debugging the autograder, a TA re-running a
    flaky test. None should re-bill the LLM.
  end note
  aip -> api : GET /submissions/{id}/feedback
  api --> aip : 200 {stored feedback}
  aip -> aip : parse results.json and splice\nthe stored feedback in

else action = GRADE
  loop for each target with ai_needed
    aip -> aip : build prompt from the rubric,\nthe feedback guidance, the source file\nderived from identifier + kind + language,\na listing of the other files,\nand any functional_test_feedback

    note right of aip #FFF8DC
      functional_test_feedback survives the
      container being destroyed, because it is in
      the database.
      That is what makes it usable in NIGHTLY
      mode, where the rerun happens hours later
      in a fresh container.
    end note

    group Multi-provider fan-out [FR-23, FR-27]
      par
        aip -> llm : Claude: generate
        llm --> aip : response
      else
        aip -> llm : ChatGPT: generate
        llm --> aip : response
      end
      aip -> aip : compare, estimate confidence
    end

    aip -> api : POST /feedback
    api -> db : insert ai_feedback rows,\nset the comparison columns,\nSET completed_at = now
    api --> aip : 201
  end

  aip -> api : POST /submissions/{id}/ai-complete
  api -> db : UPDATE submission\nSET ai_graded_submitted_at = submitted_at\nWHERE every ai_needed row has\ncompleted_at set
  api --> aip : 200 {guard_set}
  aip -> aip : parse results.json and splice\nthe feedback onto the end
end

aip -> gs : write the new results.json
gs --> stud : feedback now visible

note over trig, db #FFF8DC
  **Retry behavior is identical to IMMEDIATE.**
  The AI phase retries inside the run, with
  backoff on transient errors and no retry on
  deterministic ones. If it still fails, the
  failure message goes into results.json and
  completed_at is set. Nothing is carried to
  another night.

  Two consequences worth stating:
  1. The rate-limit argument that makes IMMEDIATE
     risky barely applies here. NIGHTLY grades at
     3am against a serialized trigger, not during
     a deadline-hour submission spike, so the
     main driver of transient failure is largely
     absent.
  2. If the trigger cannot find or click a
     submission's rerun button, record it on
     trigger_run and surface it. Nothing was
     attempted, so tomorrow will retry it, but a
     silent trigger failure otherwise looks
     identical to "no feedback needed".
end note

@enduml
